("v7178", "entry", "v7183")
("v7183", "type", "Entry")
("v7183", "key", "text")
("v7184", "type", "String")
("v7184", "scalar", "QUESTIONS / AUTHOR What do I owe\nthe people who enter my stories? Words make worlds. Worlds teach us what to notice. Writing lets me imagine lives, choices and possibilities beyond my own. I want the worlds I make to invite attention: to people, to consequences, and to things a neat explanation can leave out. A question to practise, not a finished answer. AUTHOR A story is a way of paying attention. An invented world still makes choices about who matters, whose voice is heard, and what a reader is invited to hope for. I want to make those choices with care, while leaving room for surprise, ambiguity and a reader’s own response. Where the question becomes concrete. PEOPLE Who gets to be a person in this story? A character should have room to want, refuse and surprise me. I want to notice when I have reduced someone to a lesson, a convenient obstacle or a reward for somebody else.")
