("v7279", "value", "v7280")
("v7278", "entry", "v7281")
("v7281", "type", "Entry")
("v7281", "key", "sourceSha256")
("v7282", "type", "String")
("v7282", "scalar", "725e0629b8aa41bbccc4fadc363c29a4afa4c6bf120242feb3eb684e653edf02")
("v7281", "value", "v7282")
("v7278", "entry", "v7283")
("v7283", "type", "Entry")
("v7283", "key", "text")
("v7284", "type", "String")
("v7284", "scalar", "QUESTIONS / SOURCERER How do I build\nsoftware that earns trust? Powerful tools. Visible reasons. People who can say no. I want the things I build to be useful without asking people to surrender their judgment. Sourcery explores how software can carry an explicit account of what it does, what permits it, and how a change can be checked. A question to practise, not a finished answer. SOURCERER The magic has to show its work. A system can be impressive and still be difficult to question. I want understanding, permission and repair to be part of the design. That means making consequential behaviour inspectable, keeping authority bounded, and distinguishing a promising demonstration from a capability people can rely on. Where the question becomes concrete. PERMISSION Who gave this system the right to act? A request to help is not permission to do everything. I want the scope of an action to remain clear, especially when an agent or automation is doing the work.")
("v7283", "value", "v7284")
