("v7759", "entry", "v7764")
("v7764", "type", "Entry")
("v7764", "key", "text")
("v7765", "type", "String")
("v7765", "scalar", "TEMPLE · THE FOUNDATION A foundation for\naccountable change. Temple is a small execution kernel: software that checks proposed changes against explicit rules and authority, then records acceptance or refusal. Its philosophy is that power should remain answerable to the people and commitments it affects. The lamp and gate below illustrate proposals, permissions and a history that can be checked for tampering. They are educational models, not a production security guarantee. Watch the speculative film. I · THE DISTANCE Artificial intelligence makes ideas easier to express, combine, and execute. That is its promise, but also its danger. A suggestion can become a policy, a database migration, a financial decision, a public narrative, or a deployed system before the people affected by it understand what happened or who authorized it. II · THE QUIET SUBSTITUTIONS In ordinary software, meaning slides. Authority hides in permissions, defaults and deployment scripts — or in the simple fact that someone happened to act first. And so, without anyone deciding it: generated becomes treated asis not intended")
("v7764", "value", "v7765")
