("v7881", "type", "String")
("v7881", "scalar", "THE FIELD / A HOUSEHOLD THROUGH TIME What changes when one of us changes? Two people can welcome an arrangement today and need something different tomorrow. What happens to the work, the resources, and their willingness to ask again? Synthetic worked case · fictional participants A and B · no calibrated behavioral predictions A QUESTION TO STAY WITH When a request returns, it brings a history. A respectful refusal can leave a concern heard. A promise can create expectations that only later work fulfills. A capacity limit can leave work unfinished without showing unwillingness. This small household makes those distinctions visible over time. Compare four orientations under the same kinds of change. Adjust values, capacity and the history of responses, then inspect what the assumed rules produce. No orientation wins by definition. SET UP THE HOUSEHOLD Begin with an arrangement, then introduce change. A and B are fictional partners. Their labels do not assign sex, authority or moral standing. Keep their stated values, the material outcomes and the possibility of review separate. ")
("v7880", "value", "v7881")
