("v13484", "type", "String")
("v13484", "scalar", "READING AS DAD As Dad, ask what presence would look like here, whose room to grow matters, and what a patient act of repair might require. The underlying reading and source evidence are shared. Open the shared version ↗ FINDING THINGS OUT Can a message survive a mistake? Repeating a message can help repair a mistake, within a known limit. The interactive example has its own motion control. Scroll inside it if you need more room. A mathematical check that also rules out one proposed step To send zero, use 000. To send one, use 111. The reader of the message chooses whichever bit appears most often. Choose a starting bit, then flip one transmitted bit. The original is recovered. Flip two and the answer changes to the wrong original. This small example is different from the larger error-correcting code discussed in the research. Choose a starting bit: zero or one. Flip one of the three bits, then a second. Watch the decoded answer. ")
