("v18127", "scalar", "2692ca536be320226361e7592688c6757479255af9ffcd156ed02ea32cd14a38")
("v18126", "value", "v18127")
("v18123", "entry", "v18128")
("v18128", "type", "Entry")
("v18128", "key", "text")
("v18129", "type", "String")
("v18129", "scalar", "AARON ALDERMAN / AUTHOR A world someone else can enter. On attention, imagination and the responsibility of shaping a story. A personal essay · A working reflection, open to correction A story begins with a choice about what to notice. A gesture can become an opening; a silence can change a scene; a detail that looked incidental can ask to be seen again. I want to stay with that attention before deciding what the story must mean. The Author side of this world is about shaping an experience another person can enter. It includes the pleasure of making things up and the responsibility of knowing when invention, interpretation and an account of real events need different labels. IN THIS ESSAY Attend to a life before turning it into a story. Give the invented world something to resist. Invite a reader without assigning them a private story. Let beauty and humour have room to work. Let revision change what I thought the story was.")
("v18128", "value", "v18129")
